    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "97430bf4-bab0-44e6-98f7-b15715cd1822",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Month-to-month customers had a churn rate of 42.7%, compared with 11.3% for one-year and 2.8% for two-year contracts. Churn was also higher among fiber-optic customers (41.9%) and customers paying by electronic check (45.3%). The tenure groups show a steady decline in churn, from 52.9% for customers with up to six months of tenure to 6.6% for those with more than 60 months.\n",
        "\n",
        "These are descriptive associations. They do not establish that changing a customer's contract, internet service, or payment method would itself prevent churn.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "ba432203-067b-4ca4-bf73-1d373e7c157e",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Business Recommendations\n",
        "\n",
        "Retention teams could prioritize outreach to newer customers and customers on month-to-month contracts, especially when other risk indicators are present. The high observed churn rates among fiber-optic and electronic-check customers also warrant investigation into their service and billing experiences. Outreach costs and the value of retained customer should guide how many customers are contacted.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Limitations and Conclusion\n",
        "\n",
        "On the held-out test set, the random forest identified 440 of 561 customers who churned, and missed 121. It also flagged 407 customers who did not churn. This tradeoff matters when planning retention efforts. Feature importance and group churn rates describe patterns in this dataset. They do not establish causes or prove that the recommended actions will reduce churn. The model provides a starting point for prioritizing and testing retention strategies."
      ]
    }
